fig(section=P1, id='P1S1', heading='Figure S1. Parameter recovery (N = 1,000 simulation runs)', image='P1S1.png', width=6.5,
    alt='Paper 1, Figure S1. Recovered versus true values for θ0, τS, interoceptive baseline precision, βSM and γsig, with recovery correlations from 0.60 to 0.83, and a bar chart showing βSM × baseline-precision collinearity falling from r = 0.90 to r = 0.45 with pre-session somatic-marker estimation.',
    caption=['Parameter recovery (N = 1,000 simulation runs). Recovered versus true values for the core APGI parameters (θ_{0}, τ_{S}, Π^{i}_{baseline}, β_{SM}, γ_{sig}) with identity reference lines and recovery correlations; the final panel shows the collinearity of β_{SM} and Π^{i}_{baseline} under joint estimation and under pre-session estimation of M̂(c,a).'],
    source=['Type: simulation (data figure). N = 1,000 runs. True values sampled uniformly over the plotted ranges (θ_{0} −2 to 2 in threshold units; τ_{S} 50–450 ms; Π^{i}_{baseline} 0.5–4.5; β_{SM} 0.2–1.8; γ_{sig} 0–3.6); the ceiling stress-test values (θ_{0} = 0.50, Π^{i}_{baseline} = 1.50, β_{SM} = 1.20, γ_{sig} = 5.0, τ_{S} = 300 ms) are reported separately in Table S2. Neural SNR = 4 (linear amplitude ratio). Fitted via Bayesian MCMC (Stan); all fits met R̂ < 1.01, bulk and tail effective sample sizes > 400 and zero divergent transitions. Recovery: r > 0.80 (θ_{0}, τ_{S}); r ≈ 0.70–0.75 (Π^{i}_{baseline}, β_{SM}); r ≈ 0.55–0.65 (γ_{sig}). Pre-session estimation of M̂(c,a) reduces the posterior collinearity of β_{SM} and Π^{i}_{baseline} (r ≈ 0.45, against r ≈ 0.90 under joint estimation; lower is better). Source data: simulation code and seeds, ' + ZEN + '.'],
    comments=[('all fits met R̂ < 1.01', 'Author to confirm: convergence diagnostics (R̂, ESS, divergences) must match the code release. Sampling ranges were read from the plotted axes; confirm against the simulation script.'),
              ('r ≈ 0.45, against r ≈ 0.90', 'Text now matches the plotted panel (0.45 / 0.90). The earlier text gave 0.35 / > 0.75; confirm which run is authoritative.'),
              ('simulation code and seeds', C_ZEN)])

fig(section=P1, id='P1S2', heading='Figure S2. Parameter sensitivity', image='P1S2.png', width=6.5,
    alt='Paper 1, Figure S2. (A) Tornado plot: lowering θ0 raises ignition probability by 34 percent and raising it lowers ignition by the same amount; the other parameters have smaller, opposite-signed effects. (B) Four qualitative signatures pass in 87.6 to 96.4 percent of 10,000 Monte-Carlo runs, all above the 80 percent threshold.',
    caption=['Parameter sensitivity. (A) Tornado plot of a one-at-a-time ±50% sweep of each parameter, ranked by percent change in P(ignition); bar colour gives the direction of the effect and hatching marks the −50% change. Raising the threshold θ_{0} lowers ignition probability; raising any precision, gain or time-constant parameter increases it. (B) Monte-Carlo robustness of four qualitative APGI signatures against an 80% pass threshold.'],
    source=['Type: simulation (data figure). Monte-Carlo N = 10,000 runs (parameters drawn uniformly over the Paper 1 Table S1 ranges; 500 trials per run). Robustness: bistability 94.2%; cardiac-phase difference > 8% in 87.6%; HEP–P3b r > 0.30 in 91.8%; post-block threshold elevation 96.4%. One-at-a-time ±50%: θ_{0} most influential (±34% P(ignition)); λ_{Π} (precision decay rate, both channels) least (±2%). Source data: simulation code and seeds, ' + ZEN + '. Effect magnitudes are qualitative, not point predictions.'],
    comments=[('cardiac-phase difference > 8% in 87.6%', 'Paper 1 §A.5 reports three signatures; add the cardiac-phase signature (87.6%) to §A.5 so the two documents agree (Paper 1 Blocker 22).'),
              ('Raising the threshold θ_{0} lowers', 'Panel A was regenerated with the sign required by S_t > θ_t. Confirm the sign of each remaining parameter against the sensitivity script.')])

# ---------------- Paper 2 ----------------
P2 = 'Paper 2 — The Cortex Computes Like Water'
fig(section=P2, id='P2F1', heading='Figure 1. Four biological constraints ↔ four computational requirements', image='P2F1.png', width=6.5,
    alt='Paper 2, Figure 1. (A) Four biological constraints map through a cortical liquid-state substrate onto four APGI computational requirements. (B) Constraints satisfied per architecture: the spiking LSM/LNN satisfies four, the attractor network three, the Transformer and feedforward network two each.',
    caption=["The four biological constraints map one-to-one onto APGI's four computational requirements. Among the architectures assessed here, only the liquid state machine satisfies all four. (A) Each biological constraint — (1) high-dimensional transient dynamics, (2) fading memory (~200–500 ms), (3) nonlinear separability and (4) sparse event-driven activity — is realised through the cortical LSM/LNN substrate as one APGI computational requirement: prediction-error separation (ε^{e}, ε^{i}), the accumulation window (τ_{S} ≈ 200–500 ms), the ignition-probability sigmoid and the metabolic/allostatic threshold. (B) Constraints satisfied per architecture: the LSM/LNN satisfies all four; the attractor network fails constraint 2; the Transformer fails constraints 2 and 4; the feedforward network fails constraints 1 and 2. Constraint colours (C1–C4) are for visual mapping, not epistemic ranking. Here LSM/LNN denotes a spiking reservoir with heterogeneous time constants; continuous-valued liquid time-constant networks (Hasani et al., 2021) meet constraints 1–3 but not constraint 4."],
    source=['Type: comparative schematic. Architectures compared: LSM/LNN, attractor, Transformer, feedforward. Assessed-but-excluded architectures: S4/Mamba (state-space models with selective scan; fail constraint 4, sparse event-driven activity). The claim is comparative (best among those assessed), not a uniqueness proof. No statistics; constraint satisfaction is a qualitative capability audit.'],
    comments=[('Here LSM/LNN denotes a spiking reservoir', 'Decision D-12 resolved as "spiking reservoir". Confirm that the APGI-LNN simulations implement a spiking (LSM) rather than a continuous liquid time-constant network; if continuous, constraint 4 is not met and panel B must change.')])

fig(section=P2, id='P2F2', heading='Figure 2. Cortical-microcircuit implementation of precision and the somatic-marker pathway', image='P2F2.png', width=6.5,
    alt='Paper 2, Figure 2. (A) A six-layer cortical column with receptor and afferent assignments. (B) The vmPFC to VIP+ to SST+ disinhibition circuit at baseline and on somatic-marker retrieval, with the precision-gain equation. (C) An L5 pyramidal neuron in which coincident apical and basal input triggers a dendritic calcium plateau and somatic burst.',
    caption=['Cortical-microcircuit implementation of precision (Π) and the somatic-marker pathway (β_{SM}). (A) Laminar column with afferent inputs: interoceptive context to L1 apical dendrites (red); muscarinic (M_{1}) modulation of L2/3 PV^{+} interneurons and vmPFC→VIP^{+} projections, with nicotinic (α4β2) and 5-HT_{3} drive of VIP^{+} cells (Fu et al., 2014); exteroceptive thalamic drive to L4 spiny stellate cells (dark blue); NE/α_{2} modulation of L5 SST^{+} and thick-tufted pyramidal cells (orange); L6 thalamic feedback (blue, modulatory). (B) The vmPFC→VIP^{+}→SST^{+} disinhibition circuit: at baseline (M̂(c,a) ≈ 0), vmPFC is quiescent and SST^{+} cells inhibit L2/3 pyramidal dendrites. When a somatic marker is retrieved (M̂(c,a) high), vmPFC excites VIP^{+} cells, which suppress SST^{+} cells and disinhibit pyramidal firing. The circuit implements precision gain: Π^{i}_{eff} = Π^{i}_{baseline}·exp(β_{SM}·M̂(c,a)). The somatic-marker value M̂(c,a), scaled by the gain β_{SM}, multiplies baseline precision. (C) L5 pyramidal neuron compartmentalisation: the apical dendrite receives top-down prediction (red arrows, from higher cortical areas); the basal dendrite receives bottom-up thalamic evidence (blue arrows). Coincident apical and basal input triggers a dendritic Ca^{2+} plateau and a somatic burst (backpropagation-activated Ca^{2+} firing; Larkum et al., 1999). APGI proposes, as an untested extension, that the burst channel reports the precision-weighted discrepancy between prediction and evidence (ε^{e}/ε^{i}; §6.2, Gap 1). Proposed implementation: causal validation requires optogenetic or chemogenetic circuit dissociation (§6.2, Gap 1); subcortical pathways (thalamus, claustrum) are not transcranially accessible for direct probing.'],
    source=['Type: anatomical/mechanistic schematic (proposed). Evidence tier: Plausible (literature-grounded laminar assignments). Causal test: Protocol 5 for cortically accessible nodes; deeper nodes require invasive validation. No statistics.'],
    comments=[('§6.2, Gap 1). Proposed', 'Paper 2 Fig S1 previously cited "§5.3, Gap 1" for the same gap; both figures now cite §6.2. Confirm the section number against Paper 2.')])

fig(section=P2, id='P2F3', heading='Figure 3. Ignition as a saddle-node bifurcation in the APGI-LNN', image='P2F3.png', width=6.5,
    alt='Paper 2, Figure 3. (A) Bifurcation diagram: a stable quiescent branch, an unstable separatrix and a stable ignited branch that appears at ρ_SN ≈ 0.95, with finite onset and return jumps forming a hysteresis loop. (B) Ignition-probability sigmoids centred on S_t/θ_t = 1, steepening with precision from α_psy = 2 to 20.',
    caption=['Saddle-node bifurcation and precision-dependent ignition in the APGI-LNN. (A) Bifurcation diagram of normalised steady-state activity |y*| (y-axis) against reservoir spectral radius ρ_{res} (x-axis). Two basins are separated by the unstable branch (separatrix): the sub-threshold basin (blue, quiescent) and the ignition basin (red, sustained activity). The ignited state appears at the saddle-node ρ_{SN} ≈ 0.95 with a finite jump, |y* − y_{SN}| ∝ √(ρ_{res} − ρ_{SN}), and the quiescent state is lost at a second fold (ρ_{on} ≈ 1.02). Between the folds the system is bistable and hysteretic: an all-or-none gate whose return path lies below its onset path, so the ignition and extinction thresholds differ. (B) Family of ignition-probability sigmoids parameterised by precision Π, all centred on S_{t}/θ_{t} = 1. Higher precision (red, Π = 30) produces steeper transitions; lower precision (grey, Π = 0.3) yields graded, shallow responses. The y-axis shows ignition probability; α_{psy} is the slope of these curves at threshold, the steepness observable in behavioural psychometric functions. The predicted regime α_{psy} ≥ 10 marks settings where ignition becomes quasi-discrete; continuous evidence-accumulation models instead predict graded psychometric slopes (Ratcliff and McKoon, 2008). Values 5 ≤ α_{psy} < 10 are inconclusive, and α_{psy} is fitted on the registered intensity axis with registered guess and lapse handling (Wichmann and Hill, 2001); no published estimate of α_{psy} under APGI\'s fitting procedure exists, so the prediction remains open. The core prediction is α_{psy} ≥ 10 (falsifier < 5; see OSF H3). Model-internal sigmoid steepness (γ_{sig} = 1/τ_{σ}) is related to α_{psy} but not interchangeable with it.'],
    source=['Type: dynamical-systems illustration from a reduced one-population rate model (sigmoid gain 4, offset 0.575), rescaled so that the saddle-node lies at ρ_{res} ≈ 0.95; the full heterogeneous-τ reservoir (§4.1, Appendix A.5) has state-dependent time constants that prevent analytical closure and generate the noise-averaging relation between γ_{sig} and α_{psy}. Key values: saddle-node ρ_{SN} ≈ 0.95; second fold ρ_{on} ≈ 1.02; baseline sub-critical band ρ_{res} ∈ [0.7, 0.95]; ρ_{crit} ≈ 1 reserved for the criticality regime. Panel B sigmoids are illustrative (α_{psy} = 2, 4, 7, 12, 20). Predicted behavioural falsifier: α_{psy} ≥ 10 (falsifier < 5). Source data and code: figure script, ' + ZEN + '.'],
    comments=[('reduced one-population rate model', 'Panel A was regenerated from a reduced rate model so that the plotted fold matches its label and shows a true saddle-node with hysteresis. If the paper\'s own reservoir simulation should supply the curve, re-run it with ρ_res on the x-axis and replace the panel; keep the labels.'), ('figure script, APGI', C_ZEN)])

fig(section=P2, id='P2F4', heading='Figure 4. Visual masking as accumulation vs fading memory', image='P2F4.png', width=6.5,
    alt='Paper 2, Figure 4. (A) Accumulated signal over time for a 50 ms target and a 50 ms mask at ISIs of 30, about 90 and 180 ms: traces are identical until each mask onset; at the critical ISI a 1 ms difference decides between collapse and ignition. (B) Critical ISI falls with target precision and rises with threshold and spectral radius, all curves meeting at the reference point of 90 ms.',
    caption=['Visual masking as a competition between accumulation and fading memory in the APGI-LNN reservoir. (A) Accumulated signal S_{t} over time for a 50 ms visual target followed by a 50 ms mask after a variable interstimulus interval (ISI). Traces are identical until each mask onset. At short ISI (30 ms) the signal collapses below θ_{t} (unconscious); at the critical ISI (~90 ms) the outcome is bistable — a 1 ms change in ISI decides between collapse and ignition (threshold-level access); at long ISI (180 ms) the signal crosses into the ignition zone (conscious). SOA = ISI + 50 ms. (B) Sensitivity of the critical ISI (y-axis) to three model parameters, each normalised to its reference value (x-axis); all three curves pass through the reference point (1.0, 90 ms). Target precision Π^{e}_{target} (purple) decreases the critical ISI; threshold θ_{t} (green) and reservoir spectral radius ρ_{res} (blue) both increase it. The ρ_{res} effect is the distinguishing APGI-LNN prediction: raising ρ_{res} from 0.80 to 0.92 extends the critical ISI about five-fold (26 → 131 ms), suggesting that masking strength depends on reservoir dynamical range — a prediction testable with masking paradigms (§6.3, Gap 3). Quantitative ISI values are indicative and require empirical calibration with ≥ 500 trials per condition.'],
    source=['Type: simulation (reduced reservoir–accumulator model: reservoir state with time constant τ_{0}/(1 − ρ_{res}), τ_{0} = 15 ms, mask-driven reset; leaky accumulator with τ_{S} = 300 ms and sigmoidal self-excitation). Parameters: target and mask 50 ms; ISI swept; reference ρ_{res} = 0.90; critical ISI 90 ms by construction. Critical ISI (ms) for ρ_{res} = 0.70 / 0.80 / 0.90 / 0.92 / 0.93: 9 / 26 / 90 / 131 / 167. Distinguishing prediction: masking strength scales with ρ_{res}. ISI values require empirical calibration. Source data and code: figure script, ' + ZEN + '.'],
    comments=[('reduced reservoir–accumulator model', 'Regenerated from a reduced model so that the panels obey their own logic (traces identical until the mask; sensitivity curves meet at the reference point). The previous caption claimed 4–5× for a 1.5× increase in spectral radius, which would push ρ_res above 1. Replace with output of the paper\'s own reservoir simulation if preferred; keep the panel logic.'), ('figure script, APGI', C_ZEN)])

fig(section=P2, id='P2S1', heading='Figure S1. Schematics of the two priority neurophysiological protocols', image='P2S1.png', width=6.5,
    alt='Paper 2, Figure S1. (A) Glass-brain projections of the Protocol 5 targets at their MNI coordinates: right anterior insula (tFUS), left dlPFC, bilateral PPC and vertex sham. (B) Protocol 6 intracranial-EEG trial timeline with a 500 ms pre-stimulus window for AC1, a near-threshold stimulus and a response window, and schematic supra- versus sub-threshold propagation.',
    caption=["TMS/tFUS and intracranial-EEG protocols for testing APGI predictions. (A) Protocol 5 neuromodulation targets, projected from MNI coordinates onto a glass brain: right anterior insula (aINS; MNI [34, 14, 0]; tFUS, because focal TMS cannot reach it), left dorsolateral prefrontal cortex (dlPFC; global-ignition gating, [−44, 36, 20]), bilateral posterior parietal cortex (PPC; prediction-error encoding, [±28, −60, 46]) and vertex sham ([0, −30, 70]). Predicted dissociation: aINS stimulation disrupts interoceptive gating, abolishing HEP–P3b coupling while sparing exteroceptive P3b amplitude; dlPFC and PPC stimulation reduce global ignition (less high-gamma spread and prefrontal gamma). Deeper thalamic and claustral nodes are not transcranially accessible; Protocol 5 restricts testing to cortically accessible gating nodes (§6.2, Gap 1). (B) Protocol 6 intracranial-EEG trial timeline for ρ_{res} estimation: a 500 ms pre-stimulus window, in which lag-1 autocorrelation (AC1) estimates ρ_{res}, the network's control parameter; a near-threshold stimulus presentation (500–560 ms); and a 250 ms response window (560–810 ms). Supra-threshold trials (where accumulated signal S_{t} crosses ignition threshold θ_{t}) show activation spreading from the local responsive site to widespread frontoparietal regions with high-gamma (70–150 Hz) increases. Sub-threshold trials remain locally activated with no propagation and no gamma increase, consistent with bistable sub-threshold basin dynamics. The ρ_{res} value predicts whether a near-threshold input will ignite (high ρ_{res}) or remain local (low ρ_{res}). Protocol 6 tests this prediction with pre-stimulus AC1; causal manipulation of ρ_{res} would additionally require concurrent stimulation and intracranial recording."],
    source=['Type: protocol schematic (MNI targets on a glass-brain template, nilearn; trial timeline). Design parameters: Protocol 5 N = 61 target (minimum 48), within-participant, four stimulation targets; Protocol 6 stereo-iEEG N = 20 target (minimum N = 12). Falsifiers per OSF H11 (dissociation) and H12 (ignition triad).'])

# ---------------- Paper 3 ----------------
P3 = 'Paper 3 — Consciousness Nests Across Time'
fig(section=P3, id='P3F1', heading='Figure 1. Five-level hierarchical APGI architecture', image='P3F1.png', width=6.5,
    alt='Paper 3, Figure 1. Concentric rings for Levels 0 to 4, with inward carrier arrows (cortisol, phasic NE, ACh, fast NE), an inward top-down threshold-modulation arc and an outward prediction-error arc, beside a table of each level\'s substrate, integration window, threshold time constant, carrier and APGI parameters.',
    caption=['The five-level hierarchical APGI architecture. Concentric rings depict the nested-timescale organisation of temporal strata, from the innermost reflexive Level 0 (L0; hatched, sub-APGI) outward through the cortical levels (L1–L2; integration windows 10 ms–2 s) to the association and neuroendocrine levels (L3–L4; seconds to years). Top-down threshold modulation (κ_{down}/κ_{up}, inward arc) and bottom-up prediction error (ψ(ε), outward arc) link adjacent levels; the precision-coupling constants C_{down} and C_{up} act on Π only and are not depicted. The principal cross-level neuromodulatory carriers, drawn as inward arrows, are cortisol (L4→L3), phasic norepinephrine (NE; L3→L2), acetylcholine (ACh; L2→L1) and fast NE (L1→L0). The inset table summarises each level\'s biological substrate, integration window (τ_{int}; Paper 2, Table 1), threshold time constant (τ_{θ}, the spectral pole of Figure 2), carrier received and APGI parameters (precision Π_{ℓ}, threshold θ_{ℓ}, ignition timescale τ_{ign,ℓ}). Level 0 is sub-APGI: it carries no Π/θ/τ_{ign} and supplies sensory drive to L1 rather than igniting in its own right. Full parameter values are given in Table 1 (Paper 3).'],
    source=['Type: architecture schematic. Canonical inter-level ratio k ≈ 133 (Δ = 2.124 decades); τ_{θ} span 50 ms → ~6 months (≈ 1.58 × 10^{10} ms) → L = 5 (the boundary lies at k = 133.3, so the level count is not robust to the ratio). The framework commits to the temporal gradient, not to the exact level count. Band-to-level map (Decision D-1): τ_{int} bands follow Paper 2, Table 1; τ_{θ} follows the k ≈ 133 chain and is used for the spectral poles in Figures 2 and 6.'],
    comments=[('Band-to-level map (Decision D-1)', 'D-1 resolved by separating integration windows (τ_int, Paper 2 Table 1, canonical) from threshold time constants (τ_θ, the spectral poles). Paper 3 Table 1 must adopt the same two columns.')])

fig(section=P3, id='P3F2', heading='Figure 2. Predicted 1/f composite spectrum', image='P3F2.png', width=6.5,
    alt='Paper 3, Figure 2. Log–log power spectra: five Lorentzians with corner frequencies from 3.2 Hz to 1.0 × 10^-8 Hz, their sum (black) with mean slope −1.01 and about 5 dB ripple, and the Levels 0–2 partial sum (grey), with the routine-EEG band shaded and corner frequencies marked.',
    caption=['Predicted 1/f composite spectrum from hierarchical timescale nesting. The spectrum arises from superposing geometrically spaced Ornstein–Uhlenbeck (OU) processes, one per APGI level: the threshold process θ_{ℓ} of Levels 1–4 and the gain process of sub-APGI Level 0, with spectral poles at the threshold time constants τ_{θ,ℓ}. Coloured dashed curves represent single-level Lorentzian spectra for Levels 0–4 with time constants τ_{ℓ} = {50 ms, 6.6 s, 15 min, 1.4 d, 6 mo} (common geometric ratio ≈ 133). Their superposition (S_{total}; thick black curve, the exact sum) approximates a power law across roughly 7 frequency decades, with ≈ 5 dB peak-to-peak ripple at the inter-level ratio of 133; the corner frequencies are detectable departures from scale-freedom and serve as the falsifiable markers. With equal-variance poles, as plotted, the mean slope is β = 1.01 (H ≈ 1.0 in the fGn convention); the predicted α_{DFA} ≈ 0.85–0.95 requires level variances weighted ∝ τ^{−0.2} (β ≈ 0.81, H ≈ 0.90). The grey curve shows the partial hierarchy accessible to EEG amplitude-envelope sampling (Levels 0–2 only). Vertical lines mark corner (crossover) frequencies f_{c,ℓ} = 1/(2πτ_{ℓ}): solid lines denote empirically testable transitions at ~3.2 Hz (L0/L1, standard EEG) and ~0.024 Hz (L1/L2, ≥ 30-min recording); dotted lines show predicted future-programme transitions at ~1.8 × 10^{−4} Hz (L2/L3) and ~1.3 × 10^{−6} Hz (L3/L4). The routine-EEG observable band (0.01–100 Hz) is shaded. Spectral parameters (time constants, corner frequencies) are conditional on the five-level hierarchy; other timescale architectures would yield different corner-frequency positions. This is an illustrative pre-data schematic; empirical validation requires long-horizon EEG recordings spanning the full decade range.'],
    source=['Type: illustrative pre-data schematic (the paper\'s primary quantitative signature), computed analytically from the stated time constants. Prediction: broadband H = α_{DFA} ≈ 0.85–0.95 (fGn), which requires variance weights ∝ τ^{−0.2}; equal-variance poles (plotted) give β = 1.01. Must-pass falsifier (OSF H8): α_{DFA} < 0.70 in broadband continuous recordings long enough to sample Level 2 (≥ 30 min), in healthy adults, across two datasets; or the ~3.2 Hz crossover absent. Trial-based EEG samples only Levels 0–2 and is tested against the partial-hierarchy band. Corner frequencies verified: f_{c} = 1/(2πτ) for τ ≈ {50 ms, 6.6 s, 15 min, 1.4 d, 6 mo} = {3.2 Hz, 0.024 Hz, 1.8 × 10^{−4} Hz, 1.3 × 10^{−6} Hz, 1.0 × 10^{−8} Hz}. Source data and code: figure script, ' + ZEN + '.'],
    comments=[('Must-pass falsifier (OSF H8)', 'Confirm the reworded H8 falsifier (continuous ≥ 30-min recordings; trial-based data tested against the partial band) against the OSF text before freezing. The OSF governs on conflict.'),
              ('weighted ∝ τ^{−0.2}', 'Decision D-2: numeric H is now derived rather than asserted. The paper text should state the τ^−0.2 weighting (or drop numeric H) to match.')])

fig(section=P3, id='P3F3', heading='Figure 3. Two cross-level neuromodulatory pathways', image='P3F3.png', width=5.4,
    alt='Paper 3, Figure 3. Pathway A (fast, blue): ACC drives locus coeruleus, whose phasic noradrenaline lowers the Level-1 threshold and raises Level-0 gain. Pathway B (slow, red): goal states recruit the PVN and HPA axis, and cortisol modulates Level-1 and Level-0 responsiveness bidirectionally.',
    caption=['Two cross-level neuromodulatory pathways link slow hierarchical levels (L3–L4) to fast perceptual processing (L1) and the reflexive substrate (L0). Pathway A (fast, blue; ~100 ms timescale): the anterior cingulate cortex (ACC; Level-3 node) detects goal salience, conflict or surprise and drives the locus coeruleus (LC; a Level-3/brainstem interface node) via glutamatergic cortico-coeruleus projections. This triggers phasic norepinephrine (NE) burst release (~100 ms duration), the L3→L2 carrier of Figure 1, whose net effect lowers the Level-1 detection threshold (θ_{1}↓) and increases Level-0 gain (gain_{0}↑), enhancing rapid orienting and reflexive responsiveness. In the formal model this influence is a composition of nearest-neighbour couplings (L3→L2→L1); the arrow shows the net L3→L1 effect. Pathway B (slow, red; minutes-to-hours timescale): Level-3 and Level-4 goal states and set-points (e.g., long-term goals, stress appraisal, homeostatic set-points) recruit the paraventricular nucleus (PVN; hypothalamic Level-3/4 node), which integrates inputs from limbic and brainstem regions. The PVN activates the hypothalamic–pituitary–adrenal (HPA) axis via corticotropin-releasing hormone (CRH), triggering anterior-pituitary ACTH release and subsequent cortisol secretion from the adrenal cortex (minutes to hours). Cortisol modulates L1/L0 synaptic excitability bidirectionally: acute, low-concentration cortisol (minutes; mineralocorticoid-receptor-dominated) facilitates L1/L0 responsiveness, whereas sustained, high-concentration cortisol (hours; glucocorticoid-receptor-dominated) suppresses it (de Kloet et al., 2005; Joëls, 2006). Formally, this is likewise a composition of nearest-neighbour couplings (L4→L3→L2→L1; §3.4), with delayed onset and sustained action. Together, the pathways provide coordinated threshold and gain modulation at L1/L0: Pathway A enables rapid, context-sensitive orienting (phasic NE); Pathway B sets the sustained baseline of reactivity (cortisol). Direct causal evidence for specific projection strengths and synaptic weights is limited; assignments follow established anatomical and pharmacological literature. The diagram is illustrative of the principal neuromodulatory mechanisms; additional pathways and non-nearest-neighbour influences (e.g., thalamic relays, direct cortico-cortical modulation) exist but are omitted for clarity. Timescale key: integration windows per Paper 2, Table 1.'],
    source=['Type: anatomical pathway schematic. Evidence tier: Plausible/Speculative (projection strengths from literature, not measured). No statistics.'])

fig(section=P3, id='P3F4', heading='Figure 4. Psychiatric and DoC phenotypes as level-specific dysregulation', image='P3F4.png', width=6.5,
    alt='Paper 3, Figure 4. Three columns (MDD, schizophrenia, disorders of consciousness) across Levels 0 to 4, with arrows for threshold and precision changes and crosses marking disrupted cross-level coupling; MDD shows HPA-driven threshold elevation cascading down from Level 4.',
    caption=['Psychiatric and disorders-of-consciousness phenotypes as patterns of level-specific and cascading APGI dysregulation. Columns show major depressive disorder (MDD), schizophrenia and disorders of consciousness (DoC) across Levels 0–4. Red arrows mark threshold changes (θ↑, θ↑↑; θ↓ in MCS), blue arrows mark precision changes (Π↑, Π↓), and orange crosses on dashed connectors mark disrupted cross-level coupling (κ_{k,k+1}); grey double arrows mark normative bidirectional coupling. Bold outlines mark the primary dysregulated level. MDD: HPA-driven threshold elevation at L4 cascading downward through increased coupling (κ_{4,3}, κ_{3,2}, κ_{2,1}). Schizophrenia: L1 precision elevation with disrupted L1–L2 and L2–L3 coupling. DoC: L1 ignition failure (VS/UWS) with partial restoration in MCS.'],
    source=['Type: heuristic taxonomy schematic. Only MDD, schizophrenia and DoC are developed as falsifiable cases; the mapping of more than 40 disorders (Table S4) is illustrative, not confirmed. No statistics.'])

fig(section=P3, id='P3F5', heading='Figure 5. Protocol 7 (disorders-of-consciousness) study design', image='P3F5.png', width=6.5,
    alt='Paper 3, Figure 5. (A) Predicted PCI by HEP distribution for VS/UWS, MCS, EMCS and healthy controls, ordered along a diagonal gradient, with PCI* = 0.31 separating VS/UWS from MCS and dotted ordinal cut-points. (B) Every group receives HEP, PCI and CRS-R at baseline, 3 months and 6 months.',
    caption=['Protocol 7 (disorders-of-consciousness) study design and biomarker stratification. (A) Predicted biomarker joint distribution. The two-dimensional space defined by perturbational complexity index (PCI; x-axis) and heartbeat-evoked-potential amplitude (HEP; y-axis) shows predicted group separation for four enrolled cohorts: vegetative state/unresponsive wakefulness syndrome (VS/UWS; N = 30), minimally conscious state (MCS; N = 30), emergence from MCS (EMCS; N = 20) and healthy controls (N = 30). Ellipses denote group mean ± 1 standard deviation. The vertical dashed line marks the empirical cut-off PCI* = 0.31 (Casarotto et al., 2016). Dotted lines mark illustrative cut-points of the ordinal joint model. The metric of record is the cross-validated McFadden ΔR² ≥ 0.05 of the joint HEP + PCI model over the best univariate model for baseline consciousness level (four-group ordinal); the joint C-index (≈ 0.80) is secondary. The intermediate position of EMCS is essential for validating the ordinal progression from predicted ignition failure (VS/UWS) through partial recovery (MCS) to emergence from MCS (EMCS), and for testing whether HEP and PCI provide statistically independent prognostic information. (B) Longitudinal assessment protocol. All four groups undergo identical assessment at three time points: baseline (week 0), 3 months (± 2 weeks) and 6 months (± 2 weeks). At each time point participants receive (i) EEG with heartbeat-evoked potentials (EEG/HEP), (ii) TMS-EEG with PCI calculation and (iii) Coma Recovery Scale–Revised (CRS-R) assessment. Values are illustrative pre-data predictions; this panel is rendered from the same master file as Protocols Figure P7.'],
    source=['Type: study-design and predicted-result schematic. N = 110 total (VS/UWS 30, MCS 30, EMCS 20, controls 30); TMS-EEG/PCI in all participants at all time points. Metric of record (Pred 7.A): cross-validated McFadden ΔR² ≥ 0.05 over the best univariate model (four-group ordinal), 95% BCa CI lower bound > 0; joint C-index ≈ 0.80 secondary (univariate ≈ 0.70); patient-only (three-group) model as mandatory sensitivity analysis. Falsifier: ΔR² 95% BCa CI includes 0. Acknowledged power ≈ 0.70 (moderately powered); confirmatory only after a pre-registered sample-size expansion. Evidence tier: Plausible. Reference: OSF pre-registration; corresponds to Protocol 7.'],
    comments=[('TMS-EEG/PCI in all participants', 'The earlier source data gave a PCI subsample of N ≈ 50 while the caption and panel B gave all 110 participants. Confirm against OSF Protocol 7 and amend both figures together if a subsample is registered.'),
              ('Falsifier: ΔR² 95% BCa CI includes 0', 'Falsifier restated to match the OSF harmonised metric of record (Pred 7.A). Confirm the wording against the OSF text.')])

fig(section=P3, id='P3F6', heading='Figure 6. Predicted developmental × spectral trajectory', image='P3F6.png', width=6.5,
    alt='Paper 3, Figure 6. Predicted rise of the amplitude-envelope DFA exponent from about 0.55 at birth to about 0.90 in adulthood, with inflections at about 2, 7, 13 and 25 years marking maturation of Levels 1 to 4; no empirical data are plotted.',
    caption=['Predicted developmental × spectral trajectory: maturation of long-range temporal correlations across the APGI hierarchy. The amplitude-envelope DFA exponent α_{DFA} of alpha/beta oscillations (blue curve) is predicted to rise stepwise from a neonatal baseline (α_{DFA} ≈ 0.55, near the uncorrelated value of 0.5) toward an adult steady state (α_{DFA} ≈ 0.90) as each temporal hierarchy level matures in fixed sequence: Level 1 at ~2 years, Level 2 at ~7 years, Level 3 at ~13 years and Level 4 at ~25 years. Level 0 (reflexive substrate) is presumed mature at birth, establishing the neonatal baseline. The specparam aperiodic exponent is a different quantity: it decreases (the spectrum flattens) across infancy and childhood (Schaworonkow and Voytek, 2021; Cellier et al., 2021; Hill et al., 2022), whereas amplitude-envelope long-range temporal correlations rise from childhood to about 25 years of age and then plateau (Smit et al., 2011). Level 4 maturation at ~25 years refers to cortical regulation of the neuroendocrine level, not to the HPA axis itself, which is functional from birth; it is anchored to the DFA plateau age reported by Smit et al. (2011). Four predicted maturational inflection points are marked with dots and vertical dotted lines. Absence of an inflection at a predicted age, or maturation out of sequence, falsifies the developmental extension.'],
    source=['Type: prediction schematic (developmental extension); no empirical data are plotted. Falsifier: no inflection at a predicted age, or out-of-sequence maturation. Evidence tier: Speculative.'],
    comments=[('amplitude-envelope DFA exponent α_{DFA}', 'Decision D-6 resolved: the y-axis is recast from the aperiodic exponent to amplitude-envelope DFA, which aligns the prediction with published developmental data. Paper 3 H3 and its text must be recast the same way.')])

# ---------------- Paper 4 ----------------
P4 = 'Paper 4 — Tiers, Bridges and Benchmarks'
fig(section=P4, id='P4F1', heading='Figure 1. Three-tier epistemic architecture and canonical downward bridges', image='P4F1.png', width=6.5,
    alt='Paper 4, Figure 1. (A) Three epistemic tiers with their currencies and admissible evidence. (B) The Tier 3 to Tier 2 approximate-inference bridge and the uncalibrated κ bridge from Tier 2 to Tier 1, with erased bits feeding only a Landauer consistency check. (C) The four-step double-bridge chain from Tier 3 to an empirical comparison with 0.10–0.12 J.',
    caption=['Three-tier epistemic architecture and canonical downward bridges. (A) The three epistemic tiers, with the currency and admissible evidence of each. (B) Bridges between tiers: the Tier 3→Tier 2 approximate-inference bridge (unconverged in vivo) and the Tier 2→Tier 1 κ bridge, which converts ignition-event information (N_{event}) into ATP and is uncalibrated. Bits erased at accumulator reset (N_{erase} ≈ 13–27) enter only Landauer\'s consistency check. Upward bridges are deliberately unspecified. (C) The Tier 3→Tier 1 double-bridge chain — (0) Tier-3 precision-weighted prediction error, via the Tier 3→2 bridge; (1) bits per ignition event (a Tier-2 count not yet measured); (2) × κ (ATP per bit, uncalibrated) × energy per ATP; (3) comparison with the 0.10–0.12 J model estimates of event energy (BOLD-calibrated and spike-count) — which is composite and currently unvalidated; the Landauer floor enters only as a separate erasure-consistency check.'],
    source=['Type: architecture schematic. Bridge quantities: Landauer minimum 2.97 × 10^{−21} J/bit at 310 K; κ uncalibrated (sensitivity 3 × 10^{3}–10^{9} ATP/bit; Paper 1, §4.6). Phenomenal level (Φ) excluded by design (scope = access, not phenomenal). No statistics. Overhead factors (distinct referents, not interchangeable), as in Paper 1, Figure 3: whole-event erasure ratio ~{R}_{L} ≈ 1–3 × 10^{18} (single ignition referred to N_{erase} ≈ 13–27 erased bits); per-operation bound ≈ 4 × 10^{12} (~10^{7} irreversible synaptic operations); scene-content bound ≈ 4 × 10^{13} (~10^{6} scene-content bits). See Paper 4, Table 2 (register of record).'],
    comments=[('as in Paper 1, Figure 3', 'The previous version listed a whole-brain synaptic overhead of 10^13–10^14 (Attwell & Laughlin, 2001) instead of the per-operation and scene-content bounds. Both figures now carry one set; confirm that Paper 4 Table 2 lists the same three factors.')])

fig(section=P4, id='P4F2', heading='Figure 2. Comparative audit — seven criteria × four frameworks', image='P4F2.png', width=6.5,
    alt='Paper 4, Figure 2. (A) Grouped bars of mean criterion scores (0 to 2) for GNWT, IIT (3.0 scoring), PP/FEP and APGI (self-audit) across seven criteria, with an acceptance threshold at 1.1 and explicit zeros for IIT on C2 and C7. (B) Each framework\'s primary falsification gap and the tier where it lies.',
    caption=['Comparative audit of four consciousness frameworks. (A) Grouped bars show mean criterion scores on a 0–2 scale (0 = absent, 1 = partial, 2 = strong); bar heights are means across the sub-items scored for each criterion (§5.3). Frameworks: Global Neuronal Workspace Theory (GNWT, light blue), Integrated Information Theory scored against its 3.0 formulation (IIT 3.0, orange), Predictive Processing/Free Energy Principle (PP/FEP, grey) and APGI (purple diagonal hatch, self-audit), across seven rubric criteria: C1 Tier Transparency, C2 Bridge Principles, C3 Quantitative Benchmarks, C4 Falsification Conditions, C5 Alternative Comparison, C6 Evolutionary Plausibility and C7 Causal Roadmap (§6.6). IIT scores of 0 (C2, C7) are drawn explicitly. The dashed line marks the provisional-acceptance threshold (1.1/2.0 = 55/100 on the canonical scale; §5.3). The vertical bar on APGI\'s C3 indicates the tier-resolved range (Tier 1 minimum, Tier 3 maximum), not a confidence interval, reflecting APGI\'s reported internal heterogeneity across tiers. APGI scores are self-audit values (§7.2–7.3), assigned with the same rubric the author applied to the competitors; no independent scoring has been performed. (B) Each framework\'s primary falsification gap: GNWT (Tier 3 — ignition threshold not derived from precision or metabolic cost), IIT 3.0 (Tier 2 — Φ uncomputable at biological scale), PP/FEP (Tier 3 — no conscious-access gating criterion), APGI (Tier 1 — bridge parameter κ, ATP per bit, uncalibrated). The tier designation indicates where each framework\'s evidential gap primarily resides, guiding future empirical priorities.'],
    source=['Type: audit bar chart (author-assigned scores, 0–2 per criterion). APGI values are self-audit (independence caveat: the framework proposes and scores itself). Mean scores across the seven criteria: GNWT 1.46; IIT 3.0 0.64; PP/FEP 1.27; APGI 1.52. Threshold 1.1/2 (= 55/100). No inferential statistics.'],
    comments=[('scored against its 3.0 formulation', 'Decision D-7 open: the series anchors IIT 4.0 (Albantakis et al., 2023) elsewhere. Either rescore against IIT 4.0 or add one sentence in Paper 4 §5.3 explaining why the 3.0 formulation is audited.'),
              ('Mean scores across the seven criteria', 'Means computed from the bar heights measured in the previous artwork. Confirm against the scoring sheet.')])

fig(section=P4, id='P4F3', heading="Figure 3. APGI's twelve falsifiable predictions by tier", image='P4F3.png', width=5.3,
    alt="Paper 4, Figure 3. Table of twelve predictions grouped by tier (P1–P4 computational, P5–P8 information-theoretic, P9–P12 thermodynamic), each with measurement protocol, target effect size, falsification criterion, feasibility horizon and validation status.",
    caption=["APGI's twelve falsifiable predictions, organised by epistemic tier. Predictions are grouped into Tier 3 (computational; P1–P4; blue — threshold and gain mechanisms), Tier 2 (information-theoretic; P5–P8; green — information flow and bandwidth) and Tier 1 (thermodynamic; P9–P12; vermilion — energy cost and efficiency). For each prediction, the measurement protocol specifies the design required for testing; the target effect size gives the quantitative threshold for success; the falsification criterion states the outcome that would disprove the prediction; the horizon indicates the testing timeline (now = immediately feasible; 2–5 years = near-term; 5–10 years = medium-term); and the right-hand marker gives validation status (filled = theoretically specified; open = pending empirical measurement). All predictions are anchored to the canonical access criterion S_{t} = Π^{e}·|z^{e}| + Π^{i}_{eff}·|z^{i}| > θ_{t}; Tier-1 predictions additionally require the κ bridge and the allostatic θ_{t} dynamics. Each tier is tested independently. Derivations: §7.4. P5 targets ΔMI ≥ 0.1 bit on a binary stimulus set, below the 1-bit ceiling set by stimulus entropy; P4 leaves AUC 0.64–0.80 as an explicitly inconclusive zone; P6 targets the 10–50 bits/s range, which brackets the ≈ 10 bits/s estimate of human behavioural throughput (Zheng and Meister, 2025)."],
    source=['Type: prediction matrix. 12 predictions across tiers; feasibility now (Tier 3) → 5–10 years (Tier 1). Effect sizes and falsifiers per prediction correspond to the OSF register (H1–H15) and Supplementary Table S4 (Protocols). Crosswalk to protocol predictions: P2 → Pred 6.C (OSF H12); P11 → Pred 4.A–4.C (OSF H6); P12 → Paper 1, §4.6 calibration (κ is not an OSF falsifier). The remaining predictions are programme-level and are not registered as protocol predictions.'],
    comments=[('ΔMI', 'P5 target set to ΔMI ≥ 0.1 bit on a binary stimulus set (H(S) = 1 bit), because the previous ≥ 1 bit target was unattainable. Confirm or replace with the stimulus set and value intended.'),
              ('Crosswalk to protocol predictions', 'Partial crosswalk built from the protocol figures. Confirm each mapping and whether any of P1, P3–P10 is registered.')])

# ---------------- Protocols ----------------
PR = 'Protocols'
fig(section=PR, id='PRF1', heading='Figure P0. Protocol 0 — HEP proxy validation', image='PRF1.png', width=6.5,
    alt='Protocols, Figure P0. (A) HEP amplitude correlates with heartbeat-discrimination d′ at r ≈ 0.40 in 115 participants. (B) Physostigmine raises HEP amplitude relative to placebo in a crossover (40 participants shown). (C) Within one participant, trial-wise anterior-insula BOLD tracks HEP amplitude at r ≈ 0.30.',
    caption=['Protocol 0 (HEP Proxy Validation): predicted validation of heartbeat-evoked-potential (HEP) amplitude as a proxy for interoceptive precision (Π^{i}). (A) HEP amplitude (250–400 ms post-R) correlates with an orthogonal behavioural interoceptive-precision index (heartbeat-discrimination d′); the proxy is retained only if r > 0.35 (Pred 0.A). (B) Physostigmine (cholinergic probe) raises HEP amplitude by ≥ 15% relative to placebo in a double-blind crossover (Pred 0.B), with the cardiac-field artefact controlled by ECG-regressed HEP and heart-rate matching; 40 of 115 participants are shown for clarity. (C) Trial-by-trial anterior-insula (aINS) BOLD tracks HEP amplitude at r > 0.30 after arousal control (Pred 0.C); one representative participant\'s trials are shown, because the registered test is within-participant. All three thresholds must pass for HEP to retain confirmatory status in downstream protocols. If exactly one sub-prediction fails, HEP is retained as an exploratory proxy only; if two or more fail, the proxy is disconfirmed and every HEP-dependent claim (Protocols 1, 3 and 4) is reclassified as exploratory. Values are illustrative pre-data predictions.'],
    source=['Type: predicted-result schematic (prerequisite gate). N = 115 (90% power to detect r = 0.35, α = 0.01, two-tailed). Thresholds: r > 0.35; HEP ↑ ≥ 15% (d ≥ 0.50, BF_{10} ≥ 100); aINS r > 0.30. Falsifier (OSF H2): ≥ 2/3 sub-predictions fail → HEP proxy disconfirmed, dependent claims reclassified exploratory.'],
    comments=[('If exactly one sub-prediction fails', 'The OSF carries five versions of the Protocol 0 gate rule. Confirm this wording against the harmonised OSF text before freezing.')])

fig(section=PR, id='PRF2', heading='Figure P1. Protocol 1 — Cardiac-EEG trial timeline', image='PRF2.png', width=6.5,
    alt='Protocols, Figure P1. ECG trace with ECG-anchored systole and diastole windows and the HEP epoch on the preceding heartbeat; below, a systolic trial and a diastolic trial with Gabor onset in each phase and the P3b epoch drawn 250–500 ms after the stimulus.',
    caption=['Protocol 1 (Cardiac-EEG) trial-timeline schematic. Trial structure, time-locked to the cardiac R-peak, for the interoceptive-focus, exteroceptive-focus and dual-task conditions. Trial timing and events are identical across conditions; they differ only in attentional instruction. Cardiac phases are ECG-anchored per participant: systole runs from R + 250 ms to the end of the T-wave, and diastole from T-wave end + 100 ms to the next R − 100 ms. The Gabor patch (QUEST-titrated) is presented in systole on systolic trials and in diastole on diastolic trials. The HEP epoch (250–400 ms post-R, measured on the heartbeat preceding stimulus onset; Π^{i}_{eff} measurement window) and the P3b epoch (250–500 ms post-stimulus; S_{t} > θ_{t} ignition-detection window) are marked. Key events: R-peak detection, cardiac-phase assignment, Gabor-patch onset, response window and inter-trial interval.'],
    source=['Type: trial-design schematic. N = 62 target (minimum 48; 70% power at the minimum); Bayesian sequential design with interim analyses at N = 30, 50 and 60. Powered for d = 0.45 at α = 0.0125 (Bonferroni-corrected), 80% (N = 59 required; 83% at N = 62). Falsifiers (OSF H1/H3/H4): no phase × condition interaction; α_{psy} < 5; interoceptive R² < 0.02.'],
    comments=[('no phase × condition interaction', 'The previous source data read "phase × valence"; no valenced stimuli are described. Replaced with the registered "phase × condition" (as in Paper 1 Fig 5). Confirm against OSF H1.')])

fig(section=PR, id='PRF3', heading='Figure P2. Protocol 2 — Somatic agent simulation', image='PRF3.png', width=6.5,
    alt='Protocols, Figure P2. (A) Probability of the optimal choice over 300 trials for full APGI and three lesioned agents against a random baseline, with the human Iowa Gambling Task convergence band at 50–80 trials. (B) Cross-correlation peaking at lag −2, so the somatic marker leads ignition. (C) Action-selection entropy rises after ignition. (D) APGI has the lowest BIC.',
    caption=['Protocol 2 (Somatic-AgentSim): predicted adaptive advantage of somatic markers in active-inference agents. (A) Learning curves (probability of choosing the optimal arm, 10-trial moving average) for four agents — full APGI, β_{SM} lesion, Π^{i} lesion and learning-rate lesion — against a random baseline on a volatile three-armed bandit; full APGI converges within 50–80 trials, the range over which healthy participants shift to advantageous decks in the four-deck Iowa Gambling Task (IGT; Bechara et al., 1997), while the β_{SM}-lesion agent needs 150+ trials (Pred 2.A, 2.D). The comparison is across tasks (three-armed bandit versus four-deck IGT). (B) Cross-correlation of somatic-marker retrieval M̂(c,a) against the ignition indicator B_{t}, peaking at negative lag: M̂(c,a) leads threshold crossing by ≥ 1 trial (Pred 2.C). (C) Post- versus pre-ignition action-selection entropy increases, indexing behavioural flexibility (Pred 2.F-flex, exploratory). (D) Model fit to human IGT choice sequences: APGI\'s Bayesian information criterion (BIC) on the fitting sample is lower than that of Standard PP and GNWT-only by ΔBIC ≥ 10 (very strong evidence; Kass and Raftery, 1995) (Pred 2.E). Values are illustrative pre-data predictions.'],
    source=['Type: simulation + model comparison (predicted). Agents N = 1,000 seeds per condition; human IGT fit N = 80 (hierarchical Bayesian, R̂ ≤ 1.01, BF_{10} ≥ 100 target). Predictions: convergence 50–80 vs 150+ trials; M̂(c,a) leads B_{t} by ≥ 1 trial in ≥ 75% of events; ΔBIC ≥ 10 over PP/GNWT.'],
    comments=[('on the fitting sample', 'Competing-Theories Decision D-4: confirm whether Pred 2.E is registered as in-sample ΔBIC or a cross-validated metric.')])

fig(section=PR, id='PRF4', heading='Figure P3. Protocol 3 — Anticipation fMRI', image='PRF4.png', width=6.5,
    alt='Protocols, Figure P3. (A) vmPFC–aINS coupling is higher during anticipation than at outcome, while vmPFC–pIC coupling is flat. (B) vmPFC BOLD tracks option expected value, not sensory contrast. (C) vmPFC–aINS coupling is present with a long foreperiod and absent without one.',
    caption=['Protocol 3 (Anticipation-fMRI): predicted vmPFC–anterior-insula anticipatory coupling. (A) vmPFC–aINS psychophysiological interaction (PPI; seed: vmPFC; precision-gain pathway) is stronger during anticipation than at outcome, whereas vmPFC–posterior insular cortex (pIC; sensory-intensity pathway) coupling remains flat across windows — the within-study dissociation operationalising Pred 3.B. (B) vmPFC BOLD is driven by option expected value (valence), not by sensory contrast (Pred 3.C). (C) vmPFC–aINS coupling is present under a long foreperiod (2000–4000 ms) but abolished without one (Pred 3.D) — dissociating anticipatory Π^{i}_{eff} retrieval from outcome-locked ε^{i} encoding. Bars show predicted group means ± predicted SEM; all values are illustrative pre-data predictions.'],
    source=['Type: predicted-result schematic. N = 40 target (minimum 36); primary PPI contrast d = 0.48: power 0.84 at N = 40 and 0.80 at N = 36; secondary correlation r = 0.40: power 0.73 at N = 40 and 0.68 at N = 36. Falsifier (OSF H5): no incremental variance over ε^{i} after arousal control (incremental R² < 0.02); vmPFC–ε^{i} dissociation r > 0.35 would falsify Pred 3.B.'],
    comments=[('Pred 3.D', 'Decision D-8 open: the OSF inverts the aINS/pIC roles in Pred 3.D and describes Protocol 3 as shock-only, while panel B plots option expected value. Correct the OSF or this figure so they agree.')])

fig(section=PR, id='PRF5', heading='Figure P4. Protocol 4 — Metabolic-state crossover', image='PRF5.png', width=6.5,
    alt='Protocols, Figure P4. (A) d′ and (B) P3b fall from rested to depleted state for high-interoceptive-load stimuli but barely change for neutral stimuli. (C) The interaction effect size stays above the 0.06 floor after adjustment for trial-level pupil and block-level RMSSD.',
    caption=['Protocol 4 (Metabolic-State Crossover): predicted allostatic elevation of the ignition threshold under metabolic depletion. (A) Perceptual sensitivity d′ plotted as a Metabolic State (rested vs depleted) × Interoceptive Load (neutral vs high) interaction: depletion selectively reduces d′ for high-interoceptive-load stimuli while sparing neutral exteroceptive stimuli (Pred 4.A). (B) The same interaction on P3b amplitude, the neural ignition proxy — disproportionate suppression of interoceptive targets (Pred 4.B). (C) The interaction survives covariation for trial-level pupil diameter and block-level RMSSD, dissociating metabolic allostatic triage (elevated θ_{t}) from generalised LC-NE arousal or fatigue (Pred 4.C). Values are illustrative pre-data predictions.'],
    source=['Type: predicted-result schematic (Tier-1-operational). N = 64 enrolled / 55 analysable, single site with a registered independent replication; d = 0.45, α = 0.0167, 80%. Falsifier (OSF H6): covariate-adjusted State × Load interaction η_{p}^{2} < 0.02 (or BF_{01} ≥ 6); confirmatory η_{p}^{2} ≥ 0.06, BF_{10} ≥ 100.'],
    comments=[('Falsifier (OSF H6)', 'The previous falsifier (Δη² < 0.02) described the supporting outcome. Confirm the corrected wording against OSF H6.')])

fig(section=PR, id='PRF6', heading='Figure P5. Protocol 5 — Insula causal neuromodulation', image='PRF6.png', width=6.5,
    alt='Protocols, Figure P5. (A) Glass-brain targets: aINS (tFUS), dlPFC, PPC and vertex sham. (B) PCI falls from 0.52 at vertex to 0.42–0.44 after active stimulation, all above PCI* = 0.31. (C) HEP–P3b coupling is abolished after aINS stimulation and preserved after dlPFC and PPC stimulation. (D) The aINS PCI reduction grows with baseline interoceptive accuracy; dlPFC and PPC effects are flat.',
    caption=['Protocol 5 (Insula neuromodulation): causal-neuromodulation targets and predicted dissociation. (A) Stimulation targets projected from MNI coordinates onto a glass brain: anterior insula (aINS; tFUS), dorsolateral prefrontal cortex (dlPFC; TMS), posterior parietal cortex (PPC; TMS) and vertex sham (TMS). (B) Perturbational complexity index (PCI), measured with a fixed TMS probe, is reduced by aINS, dlPFC and PPC stimulation relative to vertex, from an awake baseline (Casali et al., 2013); all values remain above the empirical cut-off PCI* = 0.31 (Casarotto et al., 2016) (Pred 5.A). (C) HEP–P3b interoceptive coupling is abolished under aINS stimulation but preserved under dlPFC and PPC stimulation — the core double dissociation (Pred 5.B). (D) Baseline interoceptive-accuracy (IA) × site interaction: high-IA participants show the largest aINS-driven PCI reduction, absent for dlPFC and PPC (Pred 5.C–D); tertile means equal the panel B reductions. Values are illustrative pre-data predictions.'],
    source=['Type: predicted-result schematic. N = 61 target (minimum 48), within-participant, four stimulation targets; d = 0.5, α = 0.0125 (Bonferroni across four targets), 90% power (80% at N = 48). Target 15–25% PCI reduction. PCI is measured with a fixed TMS probe before and after each target block; in the aINS (tFUS) versus vertex (TMS) contrast, site and modality are confounded, so the dlPFC arm carries the site inference. Engagement gate mandatory (aINS 3–4 cm deep; see Protocols §Protocol 5, engagement criteria); > 20% cohort gate failure reclassifies the protocol as exploratory. Falsifier (OSF H11): no dissociation; BF_{10} ≥ 3 for a dlPFC effect on HEP–P3b coupling.'],
    comments=[('aINS (tFUS)', 'Decision D-9 resolved as tFUS only (deep TMS co-stimulates operculum; Competing-Theories G-4). Confirm against OSF Protocol 5 and name the PCI probe site and timing.'),
              ('N = 61 target', 'N raised from 44 to 61 to keep 90% power after Bonferroni correction across four targets (statsmodels, paired two-sided). The OSF sample size must be amended to match.'),
              ('BF_{10} ≥ 3 for a dlPFC effect', 'Confirm against OSF H11.')])

fig(section=PR, id='PRF7', heading='Figure P6. Protocol 6 — Ignition iEEG (all-or-none dynamics)', image='PRF7.png', width=6.5,
    alt='Protocols, Figure P6. (A) The pooled frontoparietal high-gamma distribution is bimodal, with an intermediate band at 6.5–8.5 a.u. (B) Frontoparietal channels are bimodal while occipital channels are graded. (C) Pre-stimulus AC1 rises before detected stimuli and stays flat before non-detected ones. (D) Gamma-band coherence rises by 200 ms after detected stimuli and is sustained.',
    caption=['Protocol 6 (Ignition-iEEG): predicted all-or-none ignition dynamics. (A) Frontoparietal high-gamma amplitude is bimodal in the pooled (unconditioned) distribution, with separable detected (high) and non-detected (low) states (Pred 6.A). (B) Bimodality is regionally specific: frontoparietal channels are bimodal while occipital channels are graded, and dwell time in the intermediate high-gamma amplitude range (defined in amplitude units, here 6.5–8.5 a.u.) stays < 15% of trial duration (Pred 6.B). (C) Lag-1 autocorrelation (AC1) of high-gamma rises monotonically in the 500 ms before detected stimuli but not before non-detected stimuli — the critical-slowing signature, which APGI predicts as a pre-stimulus state and which GNWT accounts with a fixed, far-from-bifurcation baseline do not (Pred 6.C). (D) Long-range gamma-band (30–80 Hz) coherence tracks detection: frontoparietal coherence is higher on detected than non-detected trials, rising by 200 ms and sustained thereafter — the primary Pred 6.D test linking APGI to the Global Neuronal Workspace benchmark. Values are illustrative pre-data predictions.'],
    source=["Type: predicted-result schematic. Stereo-iEEG N = 20 target (minimum 12); hierarchical Bayesian single-subject analysis; BF_{10} ≥ 6 per sub-prediction to confirm; BF_{01} ≥ 6 to disconfirm. Distinguishing test: pre-stimulus AC1 rise (critical slowing). Falsifier (OSF H12): bistability without critical slowing or intermediate states (Hartigan's dip test p > 0.20; AC1 flat)."],
    comments=[('primary Pred 6.D test', 'Competing-Theories Decision D-7: align the Protocol 6 prediction count (6.A–6.D here; 6.A–6.E elsewhere) with the OSF.'),
              ('here 6.5–8.5 a.u.', 'The registered "40–60th percentile" band holds 20% of samples by construction; Pred 6.B must define the band in amplitude units in the OSF.')])

fig(section=PR, id='PRF8', heading='Figure P7. Protocol 7 — DoC biomarker study design', image='PRF8.png', width=6.5,
    alt='Protocols, Figure P7. Same master panel as Paper 3 Figure 5: predicted PCI by HEP distribution for four groups with PCI* = 0.31 and ordinal cut-points, and the three-time-point assessment schedule for all participants.',
    caption=['Protocol 7 (DoC-Biomarker) study design. (A) Predicted joint distribution of the two biomarkers — perturbational complexity index (PCI, x-axis) and heartbeat-evoked-potential amplitude (HEP, y-axis) — for the four groups (VS/UWS, N = 30; MCS, N = 30; EMCS, N = 20; controls, N = 30); ellipses show group mean ± 1 SD, the vertical dashed line marks PCI* = 0.31 (empirical cut-off; Casarotto et al., 2016) and dotted lines mark illustrative ordinal joint-model cut-points. (B) Longitudinal assessment timeline: every group receives EEG/HEP, TMS-EEG/PCI and CRS-R at baseline, 3 months and 6 months. EMCS occupies the intermediate position in joint HEP–PCI space, making the four-group gradient essential for full-gradient validation. Rendered from the same master file as Paper 3, Figure 5. Values are illustrative pre-data predictions.'],
    source=['Type: study-design schematic. Design specified in Protocols §Protocol 7. N = 110 total. Metric of record: cross-validated McFadden ΔR² ≥ 0.05 over the best univariate model (OSF H14); joint C-index ≈ 0.80 secondary (univariate ≈ 0.70). Acknowledged power ≈ 0.70; confirmatory only after a pre-registered sample-size expansion.'])

